00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:54.980
But she starts the bit a little early.

781
00:34:55.780 --> 00:34:58.820
And I think that's what sells making it look so organic.

782
00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:01.220
It's really wonderful work.

783
00:35:01.360 --> 00:35:02.380
Little details.

784
00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:04.940
Phyllis, man, with the tissue work.

785
00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:06.880
So everyone gets paired up.

786
00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:15.340
Yes, we get Karen and Phyllis and Ryan and Stanley, Dwight and Jim and Michael and Andy.

787
00:35:15.560 --> 00:35:19.100
Now they're going to go out and be in cars together all day.

788
00:35:19.100 --> 00:35:20.080
I couldn't wait.

789
00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:22.560
I remember when we got the script, Jenna, at the table read.

790
00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:26.680
When I saw, like, the groupings, I was like, oh, this is going to be so fun.

791
00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:31.560
Well, there's a Ryan talking head in here that makes me laugh so hard.

792
00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:33.940
Because you are now Ryan in real life.

793
00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:34.940
I am now Ryan.

794
00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:40.700
I love it that he said he's flattered to be Stanley's second choice after pass.

795
00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:43.280
After pass.

796
00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:45.380
Stanley got to pick his partner.

797
00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:47.600
And his first response was pass.

798
00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:51.160
And his second response was, I guess he'll take the kid.

799
00:35:51.720 --> 00:35:55.300
Well, I wanted to talk really quickly about Jim's talking head.

800
00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:59.380
He holds up that photo of Dwight and Jim, like the old photo.

801
00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:04.200
Because they clearly have been on a ton of sales calls together, right?

802
00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:07.480
And it shows their history a little bit, which I love.

803
00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:14.400
But in the DVD commentary, Rain said that his hair in that photo was from 1991.

804
00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:18.620
He was on tour with an acting company doing Romeo and Juliet.

805
00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:21.400
And they all had long Elizabethan hair.

806
00:36:24.570 --> 00:36:28.910
And they sort of, they sort of, like, photoshopped it onto current Dwight.

807
00:36:29.010 --> 00:36:30.230
But they left that big hair.

808
00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:31.310
I just thought it was hilarious.

809
00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:33.650
I love that detail.

810
00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:34.710
I know.

811
00:36:35.290 --> 00:36:37.230
So now they're all in the parking lot.

812
00:36:37.370 --> 00:36:38.510
They're paired up into their teaMs.

813
00:36:38.650 --> 00:36:40.230
They're about to get in their cars and go.

814
00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:45.470
Michael tosses Dwight his dirty laundry, which is a callback to the coup.

815
00:36:45.990 --> 00:36:48.290
I love it when our show does this.

816
00:36:48.410 --> 00:36:49.850
And we have a few in this episode.

817
00:36:49.850 --> 00:36:51.610
But this is a callback to the coup.

818
00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:54.210
And now he tells them all to circle up.

819
00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:56.230
And he's going to give them their marching orders.

820
00:36:57.190 --> 00:36:57.370
Yeah.

821
00:36:57.570 --> 00:37:02.090
So then Michael says, guys, let's go out on our sales calls.

822
00:37:02.190 --> 00:37:04.410
This is, it's like the, we're the amazing race.

823
00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:07.250
And everyone's like, how are we the amazing race?

824
00:37:07.250 --> 00:37:08.150
Is there a prize?

825
00:37:08.370 --> 00:37:09.630
He's like, no, there's no prize.

826
00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:11.450
But we're the amazing race.

827
00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:13.950
And he keeps, gives everybody these team names.

828
00:37:14.590 --> 00:37:16.130
And everyone's very confused.

829
00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:18.010
And then Michael just gets frustrated.

830
00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:20.170
He's like, just go on your sales call.

831
00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:23.630
But I love that he brought up the amazing race.

832
00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:26.690
This was a really, really popular reality show at the time.

833
00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:28.350
And I was a huge fan.

834
00:37:28.650 --> 00:37:30.830
Jenna, we were so into it.

835
00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:32.970
I remember you and I watching the amazing race.

836
00:37:33.190 --> 00:37:36.190
And then Jenna, do you remember how you and I were like, we should go on.

837
00:37:36.310 --> 00:37:38.590
We should be a team on amazing race together.

838
00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:40.270
I stand by that.

839
00:37:40.450 --> 00:37:41.130
I think we should.

840
00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:43.450
I think we, we would have been amazing.

841
00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:46.210
I think we would have been a total mess.

842
00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:47.290
Why?

843
00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:48.670
Why?

844
00:37:49.750 --> 00:37:52.810
Because I think you would be like, let's go, let's go.

845
00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:54.270
And I'd be like, can we just take a break?

846
00:37:54.470 --> 00:37:55.550
Maybe we'll have a glass of wine.

847
00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:56.390
You'd be like, yeah, yeah.

848
00:37:57.890 --> 00:38:00.450
Also, I can't eat any of that gross stuff, lady.

849
00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:01.710
I can't eat the gross stuff.

850
00:38:02.110 --> 00:38:03.310
Well, this is the problem.

851
00:38:03.510 --> 00:38:06.710
I think we would both have some of the same weaknesses.

852
00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:08.970
I think we couldn't eat the gross stuff.

853
00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:14.610
And maybe neither one of us would have great upper body strength for some of the like things

854
00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:18.910
where you have to like, like toss bales of hay down a maze or something.

855
00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:21.450
How about zero upper body strength?

856
00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:22.070
Yeah.

857
00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:22.930
Like zero.

858
00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:26.530
So maybe we wouldn't have been such a great team, but we would have had fun.

859
00:38:27.110 --> 00:38:28.330
I think we would have had fun.

860
00:38:28.450 --> 00:38:31.810
I think we would have had one colossal meltdown and then a fight.

861
00:38:31.990 --> 00:38:33.090
And then we would have cried.

862
00:38:33.090 --> 00:38:35.050
And then we'd laugh hysterically.

863
00:38:35.250 --> 00:38:37.590
We'd look like crazy people.

864
00:38:37.590 --> 00:38:38.950
But we would have had fun.

865
00:38:39.110 --> 00:38:41.210
I tell you what, we would have been good TV.

866
00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:42.690
Oh, my God.

867
00:38:42.690 --> 00:38:44.110
We would have been great TV.

868
00:38:46.010 --> 00:38:46.850
OK, OK.

869
00:38:47.050 --> 00:38:50.130
Well, we actually asked Greg about this amazing race moment.

870
00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:51.510
And here's what he had to say.

871
00:38:52.190 --> 00:38:56.290
The comparison to amazing race was fun because it's a reality.

872
00:38:56.570 --> 00:38:59.050
We were a reality based comedy show.

873
00:38:59.330 --> 00:39:05.590
It was a lot easier for the visual look of the show to be able to try and do references

874
00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:09.410
to other reality shows rather than movies, for instance.

875
00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:17.310
So there's like a Titanic reference in Booze Cruise, but I don't think it works as visually

876
00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:21.590
well as Amazing Race reference for here.

877
00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:25.210
Yeah, it totally makes sense to me, though.

878
00:39:25.370 --> 00:39:29.450
I think Michael is such a fan of pop culture and television and film.

879
00:39:29.590 --> 00:39:35.950
Like he wants any opportunity for his documentary of this paper company, whatever this is, this

880
00:39:35.950 --> 00:39:37.370
camera crew that follows him around.

881
00:39:37.490 --> 00:39:41.130
He thinks he's on par with the entertainment of the day.

882
00:39:41.550 --> 00:39:41.870
Yes.

883
00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:42.730
Yes.

884
00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:48.310
Well, so now everyone gets in their cars and we kind of start driving around.

885
00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:54.410
And Angela, speaking of driving, we had a lot of questions about how we pulled off these

886
00:39:54.410 --> 00:39:55.210
driving shots.

887
00:39:56.070 --> 00:40:03.830
Amy Jackson, Deandra Tassone, Holly E., TJ McMillan, William Burke, and many more people

888
00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:07.990
all asked, how did filming work for the driving scenes?

889
00:40:08.090 --> 00:40:11.470
Are you guys actually driving or is there a green screen?

890
00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:14.210
What cameras were used in the car scenes?

891
00:40:14.390 --> 00:40:17.010
And are the actors really driving around?

892
00:40:17.710 --> 00:40:20.350
Well, first of all, the actors really are driving around.

893
00:40:20.510 --> 00:40:21.550
That is not a green screen.

894
00:40:21.830 --> 00:40:26.490
And then we went to Greg and we asked him to talk a little bit about what it was like

895
00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:27.870
to shoot people in the cars.

896
00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:29.230
And here's what he had to say.

897
00:40:29.630 --> 00:40:37.110
And one of the things I had noticed in production was how easy it was to shoot in a car for our

898
00:40:37.110 --> 00:40:37.430
show.

899
00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:42.350
You just basically put the two characters in the car and set up lipstick cameras and drove

900
00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:43.930
around and improvised a lot.

901
00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:50.630
And so the idea for a traveling salesman was we could get a lot of production value out

902
00:40:50.630 --> 00:40:53.870
of getting out of the office and it wouldn't be too hard for us to do.

903
00:40:53.990 --> 00:40:58.050
We just tricked out these cars and they would go off.

904
00:40:58.450 --> 00:41:02.970
So we have a little lingo alert there when Greg talks about lipstick cameras.

905
00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:10.950
Those are itty bitty tiny cameras that they would put up in our visors in the car at an

906
00:41:10.950 --> 00:41:15.570
angle and one would shoot one passenger and one would shoot the other passenger.

907
00:41:16.090 --> 00:41:19.330
And we would just literally do what Greg said.

908
00:41:19.490 --> 00:41:24.070
We would just drive around and we would have a little walkie talkie in the car and there

909
00:41:24.070 --> 00:41:29.190
would be a car either in front of us or behind us that had the crew, the director, a sound

910
00:41:29.190 --> 00:41:36.370
person, they could hear us, they could see us on a little like mobile video screen and

911
00:41:36.370 --> 00:41:39.830
they would talk to us over the walkie talkie and they would say, oh, that was great.

912
00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:40.770
We're going to do that again.

913
00:41:41.410 --> 00:41:43.550
And then we would just drive around.

914
00:41:43.750 --> 00:41:44.870
They would have police cars.

915
00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:46.250
Remember the police cars, Ange?

916
00:41:46.830 --> 00:41:51.230
Well, yeah, we had like the motor motorcycle police guys, too, who would sort of be in

917
00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:55.750
the front and we'd have this path that was cleared for us so that, you know, we wouldn't

918
00:41:55.750 --> 00:42:00.030
have to drive around people who didn't know we were filming, right?

919
00:42:00.130 --> 00:42:00.990
So it was safe.

920
00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:03.730
And we would just basically make a loop.

921
00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:07.430
Usually in one neighborhood, we just make the same loop over and over.

922
00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:13.210
Yeah, that was the whole thing that Kent Zabornak, aka Kentopedia, he would have to clear that.

923
00:42:13.490 --> 00:42:19.730
He'd have to call the highway patrol or the police and they would give us a zone that we were

924
00:42:19.730 --> 00:42:22.550
allowed to use for a certain period of time on that day.

925
00:42:23.350 --> 00:42:25.450
And we would just loop around.

926
00:42:25.770 --> 00:42:30.510
And it was always difficult in Los Angeles to find a loop that didn't have any palm trees.

927
00:42:31.230 --> 00:42:37.030
So we tend to film in the same neighborhoods over and over because clearly they found trees

928
00:42:37.030 --> 00:42:40.850
that looked possibly East Coast-ish, you know?

929
00:42:41.430 --> 00:42:41.630
Yes.

930
00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:44.070
We got to know these loops very well.

931
00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:49.390
One of the things Dave Rogers said in the DVD commentary is that this was the first time

932
00:42:49.390 --> 00:42:51.870
that we used the lipstick cameras in the cars.

933
00:42:52.210 --> 00:42:56.630
Previously, when we had done driving scenes, one of our camera operators, Randall or Matt,

934
00:42:56.770 --> 00:42:59.390
would be scrunched in the back seat with a camera.

935
00:42:59.670 --> 00:43:04.190
And, you know, I noticed the video quality was a little different on the lipstick cameras

936
00:43:04.190 --> 00:43:05.470
when I was watching it.

937
00:43:05.650 --> 00:43:10.190
It's not quite as crisp as a Matt camera or a Randall camera.

938
00:43:10.910 --> 00:43:12.530
Look at you, Miss Techie.

939
00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:14.050
You got a tech alert there?

940
00:43:14.210 --> 00:43:14.770
Look at you.

941
00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:15.370
I noticed it.

942
00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:16.350
I did.

943
00:43:16.610 --> 00:43:19.150
So let's talk about all of these cars.

944
00:43:19.390 --> 00:43:20.650
And all they're driving around.

945
00:43:21.190 --> 00:43:24.490
Well, the first scene we really see is Andy and Michael.

946
00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:30.150
And Andy is determined to use this one-on-one time with Michael to chip away at Dwight.

947
00:43:30.330 --> 00:43:30.670
Right?

948
00:43:30.910 --> 00:43:32.410
He's going to undermine him.

949
00:43:32.570 --> 00:43:33.930
He asks about the laundry.

950
00:43:34.190 --> 00:43:38.730
Michael tells him that Dwight went to corporate to try to get his job.

951
00:43:38.910 --> 00:43:42.390
And, oh my gosh, Andy is like so happy to get this information.

952
00:43:42.730 --> 00:43:43.130
Yes.

953
00:43:43.210 --> 00:43:48.130
He's going to spend this whole episode slowly chipping away at Dwight's reputation.

954
00:43:48.130 --> 00:43:48.790
Yeah.

955
00:43:49.010 --> 00:43:50.750
Well, then we see Phyllis.

956
00:43:51.070 --> 00:43:52.470
She pulls into a beauty salon.

957
00:43:52.850 --> 00:43:56.890
Her and Karen are going to get some super glam makeovers.

958
00:43:57.090 --> 00:43:58.170
We don't know why.

959
00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:01.430
It looks like when you used to go to the mall to glamour shots.

960
00:44:01.670 --> 00:44:02.410
Did you ever do that?

961
00:44:02.630 --> 00:44:04.630
I never did glamour shots.

962
00:44:04.650 --> 00:44:06.450
But my sister Emily did.

963
00:44:06.830 --> 00:44:07.170
Yes.

964
00:44:07.170 --> 00:44:08.490
I never did it either.

965
00:44:08.670 --> 00:44:10.090
But my sister Tina did.

966
00:44:10.370 --> 00:44:13.410
And they gave her this enormous hairdo.

967
00:44:13.550 --> 00:44:14.730
Like this bouffant hairdo.

968
00:44:14.830 --> 00:44:15.870
And all this makeup.

969
00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:17.870
And I think like a feather boa.

970
00:44:19.450 --> 00:44:20.590
That was common.

971
00:44:20.790 --> 00:44:21.490
That was common.

972
00:44:21.650 --> 00:44:23.550
And they would shoot you on a green screen.

973
00:44:23.690 --> 00:44:26.630
And then sort of insert different backgrounds, if I remember correctly.

974
00:44:26.950 --> 00:44:27.890
Like the Eiffel Tower.

975
00:44:28.630 --> 00:44:28.830
Yeah.

976
00:44:28.830 --> 00:44:29.110
Like they would.

977
00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:30.150
Okay.

978
00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:32.110
So that is what this makeover looks like.

979
00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:34.490
That might be like an old tech alert.

980
00:44:34.690 --> 00:44:36.050
Does glamour shots still exist?

981
00:44:36.470 --> 00:44:37.790
I don't think so, lady.

982
00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:38.750
I don't think so.

983
00:44:38.870 --> 00:44:41.710
I think the era of the selfie and the iPhone.

984
00:44:42.050 --> 00:44:43.250
There aren't glamour.

985
00:44:44.010 --> 00:44:44.830
And the filters.

986
00:44:45.090 --> 00:44:46.230
Glamour shots are no more.

987
00:44:47.230 --> 00:44:50.530
Well, then we also have Ryan and Stanley.

988
00:44:51.230 --> 00:44:55.270
And Ryan asked Stanley if he could take the lead on this sales call.

989
00:44:55.270 --> 00:44:58.090
And Stanley is like, nothing would delight me more.

990
00:44:58.370 --> 00:45:00.130
Oh, Stanley knows something.

991
00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:01.270
And he's tickled.

992
00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:02.150
He can't wait.

993
00:45:02.430 --> 00:45:03.470
Oh, can't wait.

994
00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:05.970
We will find out why in a little bit.

995
00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:14.530
So now, back at the office, while all this driving around is happening, Angela invites Pam to coffee.

996
00:45:14.790 --> 00:45:15.190
Yes.

997
00:45:15.290 --> 00:45:16.310
And Pam was like, what?

998
00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:17.550
But okay.

999
00:45:18.050 --> 00:45:22.690
Now, on a personal note, I was thrilled to do this scene.

1000
00:45:22.690 --> 00:45:27.850
You and I never got to do scenes together, let alone go on a location together.

1001
00:45:28.050 --> 00:45:29.530
We were giddy, Jenna.

1002
00:45:29.770 --> 00:45:30.710
We couldn't wait.

1003
00:45:30.970 --> 00:45:38.590
So some of that smiling and happiness you see in Angela Martin is just me being happy that I get to do a scene with my BFF.

1004
00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:40.190
And I get to be normal.

1005
00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:42.770
I get to smile and not be snarky.

1006
00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:47.370
It was so, we were so excited, Angela, when we got this script.

1007
00:45:47.610 --> 00:45:47.850
Yeah.

1008
00:45:48.170 --> 00:45:50.230
All of my scenes are basically with you.

1009
00:45:50.670 --> 00:45:52.410
Yeah, we had the same work day.

1010
00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:53.970
And then we had a few days off.

1011
00:45:54.070 --> 00:45:55.490
It was like, we were like, woo-hoo!

1012
00:45:55.910 --> 00:45:57.850
It was kind of like BFF week.

1013
00:45:58.030 --> 00:45:58.410
Yeah.

1014
00:45:58.510 --> 00:45:59.090
For us.

1015
00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:04.970
Well, some people pointed out that at the end of the scene, Pam grabs a notebook.

1016
00:46:06.230 --> 00:46:10.830
And people were wondering, why is Pam bringing a notebook to coffee?

1017
00:46:10.990 --> 00:46:12.730
Is she going to take notes on Angela?

1018
00:46:13.590 --> 00:46:14.010
No.

1019
00:46:14.650 --> 00:46:15.550
No, you guys.

1020
00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:17.050
There was a line that got cut out.

1021
00:46:17.710 --> 00:46:19.690
Pam says to Angela, you know what?

1022
00:46:19.770 --> 00:46:21.610
I'll go around and take some coffee orders.

1023
00:46:21.970 --> 00:46:24.130
I'll see if anybody wants anything.

1024
00:46:24.710 --> 00:46:25.070
Yes.

1025
00:46:25.210 --> 00:46:26.490
This is in the deleted scenes.

1026
00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:27.370
She goes around.

1027
00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:29.310
Meredith gets really angry at you.

1028
00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:32.810
She's like, why would I pay $4 for milk in a cup?

1029
00:46:32.950 --> 00:46:34.670
And she's like, it's all angry.

1030
00:46:34.670 --> 00:46:36.370
We're in Creed get real cranky, right?

1031
00:46:36.410 --> 00:46:36.610
Yeah.

1032
00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:39.130
They get real cranky about the fancy coffee orders.

1033
00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:43.150
And there is a scene, Jenna, that just won't break your heart.

1034
00:46:43.250 --> 00:46:48.810
Because when we come back with the coffee, it's clear that Toby only ordered coffee so

1035
00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:50.550
he could have some interaction with Pam.

1036
00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:55.430
But when she comes back with all the coffees and she's passing them around, she just hands

1037
00:46:55.430 --> 00:46:57.450
it to Kelly, who then takes it to Toby.

1038
00:46:58.050 --> 00:47:03.650
And Toby just looks at Pam through the glass partition from the annex, and he just puts the

1039
00:47:03.650 --> 00:47:04.590
coffee in the trash.

1040
00:47:04.750 --> 00:47:06.270
He didn't even get to talk to her.

1041
00:47:06.530 --> 00:47:07.290
Aw, Toby.

1042
00:47:07.650 --> 00:47:08.570
Poor Toby.

1043
00:47:08.930 --> 00:47:09.410
Toby.

1044
00:47:09.610 --> 00:47:10.270
So sad.

1045
00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:17.530
Well, Ange, in the script, there is a scene where Pam is taking Toby's coffee order.

1046
00:47:17.890 --> 00:47:18.630
Oh, yeah?

1047
00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:20.150
That's not in the deleted scenes.

1048
00:47:20.930 --> 00:47:21.530
Yeah.

1049
00:47:21.790 --> 00:47:23.330
And he orders a chai latte.

1050
00:47:23.650 --> 00:47:27.170
And Pam says, oh, my gosh, that's my coffee order.

1051
00:47:27.390 --> 00:47:29.590
And he's like, oh, really?

1052
00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:32.310
Oh, we have the same coffee order.

1053
00:47:33.470 --> 00:47:34.070
Yeah.

1054
00:47:34.850 --> 00:47:42.510
So not only does he not get his coffee delivered by Pam, he ordered a coffee he didn't even really

1055
00:47:42.510 --> 00:47:45.010
drink, just to have something in common with her.

1056
00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:46.530
Toby.

1057
00:47:47.670 --> 00:47:50.230
He's stalking Pam and in love with her.

1058
00:47:50.590 --> 00:47:55.230
So now, you guys, we're in the parking lot, and Dwight wants to amp himself up.

1059
00:47:55.430 --> 00:47:57.790
He's got to get pumped for this sales call.

1060
00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:00.750
Jim knows they have been on countless sales calls.

1061
00:48:00.890 --> 00:48:02.030
He's like, you still do the thing?

1062
00:48:02.150 --> 00:48:03.450
And Dwight's like, just give me the keys.

1063
00:48:03.590 --> 00:48:06.310
And then he plays like, I don't know, what is it, like Motley Crue or something?

1064
00:48:06.310 --> 00:48:12.630
And once again, we see Dwight, like rocking out, doing air guitar to heavy metal, like punching

1065
00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:14.930
the seat to get ready.

1066
00:48:15.790 --> 00:48:16.070
Yes.

1067
00:48:16.530 --> 00:48:16.930
Yeah.

1068
00:48:17.130 --> 00:48:19.410
This is his routine, I guess.

1069
00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:19.950
Yeah.

1070
00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:20.790
And Jim knows it.

1071
00:48:21.110 --> 00:48:21.970
They're like old roommates.

1072
00:48:22.090 --> 00:48:22.870
He's like, here we go.

1073
00:48:22.990 --> 00:48:24.150
I stand out here.

1074
00:48:24.330 --> 00:48:25.250
He does air guitar.

1075
00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:32.690
So he is amped up, because then when they're walking into the building, Dwight has to be

1076
00:48:32.690 --> 00:48:33.090
Dwight.

1077
00:48:33.210 --> 00:48:38.130
And he's like, you go in first, because he doesn't want to get attacked from behind.

1078
00:48:38.490 --> 00:48:40.950
Seven out of ten attacks are from the rear.

1079
00:48:41.950 --> 00:48:42.350
Right.

1080
00:48:42.490 --> 00:48:45.510
And Jim is like, well, what about the other 30%?

1081
00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:47.890
And Dwight's like, I'd block it.

1082
00:48:48.030 --> 00:48:48.910
I'd block it.

1083
00:48:49.070 --> 00:48:49.930
You can't get me.

1084
00:48:50.930 --> 00:48:57.070
And then in like mid-sentence, Jim just hauls off and smacks Dwight.

1085
00:48:57.170 --> 00:48:58.570
And he is shocked.

1086
00:48:58.670 --> 00:49:00.730
He can't believe he's been attacked from the front.

1087
00:49:01.250 --> 00:49:02.510
He can't believe it.

1088
00:49:02.650 --> 00:49:04.110
Also, he's about to break.

1089
00:49:04.310 --> 00:49:05.330
Rain is about to laugh.

1090
00:49:05.470 --> 00:49:06.970
You can totally see it.

1091
00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:10.310
At eight minutes, 50 seconds, Rain is about to laugh.

1092
00:49:10.550 --> 00:49:11.670
Oh, 100%.

1093
00:49:11.670 --> 00:49:12.610
100%.

1094
00:49:12.610 --> 00:49:14.850
Well, we had a fan question from Rachel Fortune.

1095
00:49:15.430 --> 00:49:18.650
Was Jim supposed to slap Dwight as they walk in the building?

1096
00:49:18.830 --> 00:49:21.110
Because Rain looked truly shocked.

1097
00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:25.410
Well, guys, this was a scripted moment.

1098
00:49:27.650 --> 00:49:32.930
But there's a reason why Rain was surprised, even though it was scripted.

1099
00:49:33.050 --> 00:49:34.910
And we asked Greg about it.

1100
00:49:35.130 --> 00:49:36.270
Here's what he had to say.

1101
00:49:36.730 --> 00:49:41.350
So some people asked about the Jim slapping Dwight moment.

1102
00:49:41.350 --> 00:49:48.950
So, you know, it's fun to think of the two of them as being teamed up earlier in their career.

1103
00:49:49.170 --> 00:49:57.750
And we gave it kind of an Abbott and Costello comedy team feel, you know, where Jim is like stopping short.

1104
00:49:57.750 --> 00:49:58.910
So Dwight hits his head.

1105
00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:03.810
And usually these little moments involve some comeuppance for Dwight.

1106
00:50:03.970 --> 00:50:10.690
And I remember John came up to me right before we were going to shoot the part where he hits Dwight in the face.

1107
00:50:11.230 --> 00:50:17.570
And he said that he wanted to hit Rain for real a little bit harder than they had worked out just to get a good startle out of him.

1108
00:50:17.730 --> 00:50:22.670
And I can't recall whether Rain was in on this, but just didn't want to know which take.

1109
00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:25.890
But we kind of worked out which take he was going to do.

1110
00:50:26.250 --> 00:50:32.830
And I'm pretty sure we used the one where Rain was really startled that John had hit him.

1111
00:50:34.470 --> 00:50:35.830
So here's the deal.

1112
00:50:36.110 --> 00:50:40.050
On the DVD commentary, Rain and John talk in depth about this moment.

1113
00:50:40.270 --> 00:50:43.410
And Rain asked John to hit him harder.

1114
00:50:43.650 --> 00:50:46.310
He was like, I want you to hit me hard.

1115
00:50:46.630 --> 00:50:48.170
And here's the thing about Rain.

1116
00:50:48.330 --> 00:50:49.490
He's very method, you guys.

1117
00:50:49.490 --> 00:50:52.470
When I slapped him, remember in Casino Night?

1118
00:50:52.770 --> 00:50:55.430
He said to me, Ange, you got to slap me harder.

1119
00:50:55.890 --> 00:50:56.350
Really?

1120
00:50:56.590 --> 00:50:57.070
Slap me.

1121
00:50:57.270 --> 00:51:00.490
So in the DVD commentary, John was like, remember Rain?

1122
00:51:00.590 --> 00:51:01.650
You were like, slap me harder.

1123
00:51:01.730 --> 00:51:02.490
And I was like, okay.

1124
00:51:02.870 --> 00:51:07.570
And of course, that's the take they used because Rain was legitimately like, oh.

1125
00:51:08.210 --> 00:51:09.890
And then he almost started laughing.

1126
00:51:10.370 --> 00:51:13.910
Well, you can totally tell that he was taken aback.

1127
00:51:14.150 --> 00:51:14.490
Yeah.

1128
00:51:14.890 --> 00:51:18.970
And it ends up making a scripted moment look unscripted.

1129
00:51:19.510 --> 00:51:21.210
But it was definitely scripted.

1130
00:51:21.290 --> 00:51:25.550
I remember it said something like, Dwight gets smacked in the head by Jim.

1131
00:51:25.910 --> 00:51:30.270
And, you know, we would always have stunt coordinators come in anytime we had to put our

1132
00:51:30.270 --> 00:51:35.150
hands on another actor, even if it was something that might be considered small, like a slap.

1133
00:51:35.330 --> 00:51:41.670
And they actually teach you how to hit someone with your palm in this certain sort of cupped

1134
00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:42.030
way.

1135
00:51:42.030 --> 00:51:46.590
So it makes a louder noise or it looks more violent than it really is.

1136
00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:51.370
And so, you know, we always had that training in us as well.

1137
00:51:51.490 --> 00:51:57.030
So I guess I should say Rain was not hurt in the execution of this, but he was certainly

1138
00:51:57.030 --> 00:51:57.510
shocked.

1139
00:51:58.210 --> 00:51:59.090
Well, it worked.

1140
00:51:59.090 --> 00:52:01.670
And it cracked Rain up and everyone else.

1141
00:52:01.790 --> 00:52:03.010
And that's the take they picked.

1142
00:52:03.690 --> 00:52:05.510
So, lady, where are we?

1143
00:52:05.630 --> 00:52:07.310
Are we going into some sales calls?

1144
00:52:07.810 --> 00:52:08.630
We are.

1145
00:52:08.850 --> 00:52:11.870
We're about to go into all these sales meetings, but why don't we take a break?

1146
00:52:12.030 --> 00:52:14.010
And then we'll pick up right there in just a moment.

1147
00:52:14.470 --> 00:52:14.810
Yeah.

1148
00:52:15.490 --> 00:52:24.770
All right.

1149
00:52:24.770 --> 00:52:26.010
We are back.

1150
00:52:27.010 --> 00:52:29.310
Let's talk about all of these sales meetings.

1151
00:52:30.110 --> 00:52:30.550
Yes.

1152
00:52:30.550 --> 00:52:32.950
I think the first one is Michael and Andy.

1153
00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:36.870
And Jenna, once again, we see that Michael is a good salesman.

1154
00:52:37.090 --> 00:52:38.690
He knows how to connect with people.

1155
00:52:38.930 --> 00:52:40.290
He really does.

1156
00:52:40.630 --> 00:52:41.370
He really does.

1157
00:52:41.530 --> 00:52:43.870
He doesn't know how to be someone's boss.

1158
00:52:44.810 --> 00:52:46.150
That is his failing.

1159
00:52:46.430 --> 00:52:49.870
He thinks that he's supposed to entertain you as a boss.

1160
00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:50.230
Yeah.

1161
00:52:50.350 --> 00:52:53.110
But he does know how to connect with people one on one.

1162
00:52:53.270 --> 00:52:55.930
And he really connects with this guy over fishing.

1163
00:52:56.110 --> 00:52:58.670
He compliments the fish that he caught.

1164
00:52:58.670 --> 00:53:01.590
And it's a callback to Lake Wallen-Paw Pack.

1165
00:53:02.470 --> 00:53:03.590
Love a callback.

1166
00:53:03.790 --> 00:53:04.570
Love a callback.

1167
00:53:04.750 --> 00:53:08.690
And then Andy's like, yeah, my dad has a bayliner.

1168
00:53:08.930 --> 00:53:10.950
Whatever he says, I shot a shark.

1169
00:53:11.310 --> 00:53:18.850
I mean, if you were a true fisherman, the idea of shooting a fish in any way is so off-putting.

1170
00:53:19.110 --> 00:53:21.610
There's sort of an art to fishing.

1171
00:53:21.610 --> 00:53:26.130
And a lot of people even do like the throwback, you know, if they're fishing on a lake.

1172
00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:29.510
So you could just tell this really was off-putting to this man.

1173
00:53:30.710 --> 00:53:35.350
Angela, do you know all of that because of your ex-boyfriend that made you watch Bassmasters?

1174
00:53:36.150 --> 00:53:38.310
Oh, God, you had to bring up.

1175
00:53:40.270 --> 00:53:44.310
I want to tell you, Angela, after we talked about Bassmasters on the podcast,

1176
00:53:44.310 --> 00:53:47.750
they sent us each a little goodie bag of stuff.

1177
00:53:48.010 --> 00:53:50.890
And one of the things that they sent us, it was so sweet.

1178
00:53:50.990 --> 00:53:52.830
They just said, hey, thanks for talking about us.

1179
00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:55.250
It was totally organic and random.

1180
00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:58.690
But they sent me this journal.

1181
00:53:58.970 --> 00:54:00.830
A leather-bound journal.

1182
00:54:00.830 --> 00:54:02.190
It's so nice.

1183
00:54:02.570 --> 00:54:05.570
A leather-bound journal that says Bassmasters on it.

1184
00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:06.810
And I love it.

1185
00:54:06.910 --> 00:54:08.610
It is my new favorite journal.

1186
00:54:08.610 --> 00:54:14.550
So now, because of your ex-boyfriend making you watch Bassmasters and you bringing it

1187
00:54:14.550 --> 00:54:19.230
up on this podcast, I now journal in a Bassmasters journal.

1188
00:54:19.570 --> 00:54:20.970
That is what has happened.

1189
00:54:21.330 --> 00:54:22.630
Well, you're welcome.

1190
00:54:24.010 --> 00:54:26.050
And Kyle says you're welcome.

1191
00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:27.010
That was his name.

1192
00:54:27.910 --> 00:54:29.450
Well, thank you, Kyle.

1193
00:54:29.670 --> 00:54:30.190
Oh, no.

1194
00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:31.150
It's out there.

1195
00:54:31.710 --> 00:54:32.290
Oh, boy.

1196
00:54:32.290 --> 00:54:33.230
Um, yeah.

1197
00:54:33.490 --> 00:54:36.070
Well, Bassmasters sent me a hoodie that I love.

1198
00:54:36.130 --> 00:54:37.970
And I walk around with my Bassmasters hoodie.

1199
00:54:38.550 --> 00:54:39.090
Yeah.

1200
00:54:39.290 --> 00:54:40.510
So here's the thing.

1201
00:54:40.690 --> 00:54:47.370
There's a great little moment in the DVD commentary about this scene where Ed says that he and Steve

1202
00:54:47.370 --> 00:54:50.010
did go off script in this scene.

1203
00:54:50.510 --> 00:54:57.770
And the actor that plays the man, you know, the business owner they're talking to was not

1204
00:54:57.770 --> 00:55:00.090
really ready for them to go off script.

1205
00:55:00.270 --> 00:55:00.750
He wasn't.

1206
00:55:00.970 --> 00:55:06.790
And he just gave them this sort of odd quizzical look like, what are you talking about?

1207
00:55:07.030 --> 00:55:11.090
And Dave Rogers is in this commentary and he goes, but it was perfect.

1208
00:55:11.430 --> 00:55:18.290
So that look you see this man giving Michael and Andy like what is really earneSt.

1209
00:55:18.950 --> 00:55:20.130
Oh, my gosh.

1210
00:55:20.130 --> 00:55:21.730
I love that.

1211
00:55:22.010 --> 00:55:23.810
I love that tidbit.

1212
00:55:23.990 --> 00:55:29.130
Well, guys, the actor who plays the client that Michael and Andy are talking to is named

1213
00:55:29.130 --> 00:55:30.270
Warren Sweeney.

1214
00:55:30.270 --> 00:55:34.890
So, Warren, while you were surprised, your look worked.

1215
00:55:35.310 --> 00:55:36.530
It totally worked.

1216
00:55:36.750 --> 00:55:44.310
Well, then we cut to the car and you can tell Michael is not pleased with Andy, but Andy

1217
00:55:44.310 --> 00:55:49.950
turns it around and he's like, I'm so sorry for screwing up our sales call.

1218
00:55:50.170 --> 00:55:51.330
I really shrewd at it.

1219
00:55:51.550 --> 00:55:51.730
Yeah.

1220
00:55:51.930 --> 00:55:54.470
That's what we say around the office when someone really screws up.

1221
00:55:54.570 --> 00:55:55.790
We say they shrewd at it.

1222
00:55:55.790 --> 00:55:58.450
And then he's like, I wonder, I wonder how that name came up.

1223
00:55:58.530 --> 00:55:59.670
I wonder if it's because of Dwight.

1224
00:55:59.830 --> 00:56:01.950
And Michael gives him nothing.

1225
00:56:02.330 --> 00:56:04.830
Michael's like, well, who knows how words are formed?

1226
00:56:05.070 --> 00:56:06.650
Like, he's not getting it.

1227
00:56:06.750 --> 00:56:08.790
And Andy's getting so frustrated.

1228
00:56:09.010 --> 00:56:14.230
Andy is planting all these seeds that Dwight is an idiot and Michael is not getting it.

1229
00:56:14.410 --> 00:56:15.210
It's awesome.

1230
00:56:15.210 --> 00:56:19.050
So, let's now talk about Phyllis and Karen's meeting.

1231
00:56:19.410 --> 00:56:22.810
They get a very large order from this gentleman.

1232
00:56:23.330 --> 00:56:25.990
Oh, this is my St. Louis catch.

1233
00:56:26.370 --> 00:56:27.030
All right.

1234
00:56:27.270 --> 00:56:30.950
This is around 11 minutes, 33 seconds.

1235
00:56:32.330 --> 00:56:34.950
And Phyllis goes, well, thanks.

1236
00:56:35.050 --> 00:56:35.870
That's a big order.

1237
00:56:36.170 --> 00:56:37.050
She doesn't say order.

1238
00:56:37.270 --> 00:56:38.210
That's a big order.

1239
00:56:38.350 --> 00:56:39.330
It says like order.

1240
00:56:40.170 --> 00:56:42.270
Yeah, that's right in line with FARC.

1241
00:56:42.610 --> 00:56:43.970
Oh, St. Louis catch.

1242
00:56:44.770 --> 00:56:47.830
And then she asked how his wife is doing.

1243
00:56:48.070 --> 00:56:51.750
And he shows a photo of himself and his wife on vacation.

1244
00:56:51.750 --> 00:56:53.410
And they look just like her.

1245
00:56:53.570 --> 00:56:55.530
The same hair, the same makeup.

1246
00:56:55.910 --> 00:57:00.290
And Karen's like, ah, I see what you're doing, Phyllis.

1247
00:57:00.510 --> 00:57:01.270
Very smart.

1248
00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:03.230
Okay.

1249
00:57:03.350 --> 00:57:06.270
We've got to talk about Dwight and Jim in their sales call.

1250
00:57:06.410 --> 00:57:08.750
Because Jenna, they clearly have a whole shtick.

1251
00:57:09.150 --> 00:57:09.710
Yes.

1252
00:57:10.890 --> 00:57:12.290
I loved it.

1253
00:57:12.410 --> 00:57:13.790
It's so seamless.

1254
00:57:14.390 --> 00:57:14.890
Yes.

1255
00:57:15.050 --> 00:57:18.450
I want you to know my kids laugh so hard when Dwight,

1256
00:57:18.590 --> 00:57:20.750
when he's making the call and he's going seven.

1257
00:57:21.810 --> 00:57:22.350
Three.

1258
00:57:23.170 --> 00:57:24.750
We're cracking up.

1259
00:57:25.710 --> 00:57:27.490
Well, I don't know about you, Angela,

1260
00:57:27.670 --> 00:57:34.210
but I hate those like robocall customer service lines so much

1261
00:57:34.210 --> 00:57:37.870
that I just like immediately when they come up,

1262
00:57:38.010 --> 00:57:42.250
I'm like, agent, speak to a, speak to pharmacist, agent.

1263
00:57:42.930 --> 00:57:45.050
I won't even say any numbers.

1264
00:57:45.270 --> 00:57:46.690
I'm like, I can't.

1265
00:57:46.850 --> 00:57:47.390
I can't.

1266
00:57:47.690 --> 00:57:51.170
Jenna, I have such a fond memory of my dad.

1267
00:57:51.870 --> 00:57:56.630
It's weird, you know, when you, when someone you love so much passes away.

1268
00:57:56.970 --> 00:57:59.670
And I don't mean to be a Debbie Downer here in this moment,

1269
00:57:59.670 --> 00:58:01.610
because this is a happy memory for me.

1270
00:58:01.790 --> 00:58:06.410
But it's interesting the things your brain latches onto and holds dear.

1271
00:58:06.690 --> 00:58:09.210
Things you would never, ever expect.

1272
00:58:09.210 --> 00:58:16.890
But I have such a vivid memory of my dad on the phone with clearly like some kind of like robot.

1273
00:58:17.190 --> 00:58:21.910
And he's, he's this, you know, he was just this sweet cowboy, you know, Southern guy.

1274
00:58:22.130 --> 00:58:24.850
And he was going, five.

1275
00:58:25.730 --> 00:58:26.250
No.

1276
00:58:27.130 --> 00:58:27.650
Yes.

1277
00:58:28.350 --> 00:58:29.590
Talk to person.

1278
00:58:30.170 --> 00:58:30.690
Person.

1279
00:58:34.780 --> 00:58:37.100
And then like whatever the password was.

1280
00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:38.500
Tractor.

1281
00:58:39.120 --> 00:58:39.620
Tractor.

1282
00:58:43.480 --> 00:58:45.380
Oh, I love that, Ange.

1283
00:58:45.380 --> 00:58:46.720
I love that you shared that.

1284
00:58:48.660 --> 00:58:50.760
It, it, it made me think of him.

1285
00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:51.480
It's weird.

1286
00:58:51.640 --> 00:58:53.860
It's weird the way your brain works like that.

1287
00:58:54.020 --> 00:58:55.000
Your brain and your heart.

1288
00:58:55.460 --> 00:58:55.860
Okay.

1289
00:58:56.240 --> 00:58:56.660
So yes.

1290
00:58:56.900 --> 00:58:59.160
So Dwight is doing this call.

1291
00:58:59.360 --> 00:59:00.600
We don't know who he's calling.

1292
00:59:00.940 --> 00:59:03.440
And Jim presses on with his sales pitch, right?

1293
00:59:04.460 --> 00:59:04.720
Yeah.

1294
00:59:04.840 --> 00:59:05.580
Through all of this.

1295
00:59:05.700 --> 00:59:08.760
And the guy is like, listen, I don't know what to tell you.

1296
00:59:08.840 --> 00:59:13.360
I mean, I just maybe feel more comfortable with the, with a bigger company.

1297
00:59:13.840 --> 00:59:15.040
With one of the big guys.

1298
00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:17.800
And they're like, well, is customer service important to you?

1299
00:59:17.900 --> 00:59:19.380
And he's like, it's very important.

1300
00:59:20.220 --> 00:59:26.000
And he's like, well, we've been on hold with the customer service department of a big company.

1301
00:59:26.360 --> 00:59:28.380
Oh, and there's the switch.

1302
00:59:28.820 --> 00:59:29.160
Aha.

1303
00:59:29.480 --> 00:59:30.560
There was a whole purpose.

1304
00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:35.200
And then he calls Dunder Mifflin customer service and Kelly answers.

1305
00:59:35.280 --> 00:59:36.860
I was like, hey, oh my God, what do you guys do?

1306
00:59:38.420 --> 00:59:38.860
Yes.

1307
00:59:39.580 --> 00:59:40.020
Yes.

1308
00:59:40.320 --> 00:59:41.440
So amazing.

1309
00:59:41.800 --> 00:59:43.400
And then the guy is like, sold.

1310
00:59:43.680 --> 00:59:44.920
I'll give you my business.

1311
00:59:45.340 --> 00:59:45.420
Sold.

1312
00:59:45.620 --> 00:59:47.420
And Dwight hands him his business card.

1313
00:59:47.420 --> 00:59:49.780
And Jenna, I love what's on Dwight's business card.

1314
00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:54.860
His cell number, his pager number, his home number, and his other pager number.

1315
00:59:55.120 --> 00:59:55.400
Yeah.

1316
00:59:55.600 --> 00:59:57.840
And then he says, I take no days off.

1317
00:59:57.840 --> 01:00:00.560
I've never been sick and I don't celebrate any major holidays.

1318
01:00:00.820 --> 01:00:02.720
And he's like, okay, okay, I got it.

1319
01:00:03.160 --> 01:00:04.140
Interesting tech alert.

1320
01:00:04.420 --> 01:00:07.420
His email was not on his business card, Jenna.

1321
01:00:09.080 --> 01:00:09.560
Huh.

1322
01:00:09.920 --> 01:00:11.900
Nowadays, I think your email would be on there.

1323
01:00:12.800 --> 01:00:13.760
For sure.

1324
01:00:14.040 --> 01:00:14.680
For sure.

1325
01:00:15.380 --> 01:00:17.880
But we shot this in the Stone Age.

1326
01:00:18.100 --> 01:00:21.520
So now Ryan and Stanley go into the church for their sales call.

1327
01:00:21.700 --> 01:00:27.980
And these four older, very sort of like dignified black men in their suits walk out to him.

1328
01:00:28.200 --> 01:00:30.160
They're clearly an older generation.

1329
01:00:30.380 --> 01:00:31.580
They're Stanley's generation.

1330
01:00:32.140 --> 01:00:36.980
And there's this young white kid who, Jenna, I don't think, I don't think Ryan's been in

1331
01:00:36.980 --> 01:00:37.960
the church in a while.

1332
01:00:39.200 --> 01:00:39.600
No.

1333
01:00:39.800 --> 01:00:44.880
And we've established that the way all these sales calls are successful is by people connecting

1334
01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:46.960
with their client in some personal way.

1335
01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:50.640
